{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And I will set My dwelling in your midst</b> — this is the Temple. <b>And My soul will not be repulsed by you</b> — My spirit will not feel disgust toward you. Every <i>ge'ilah</i> (repulsion) is an expression of expelling something absorbed within a thing, as in \"for there the shield of warriors was repulsed\" (II Samuel 1:21) — it did not receive the anointing with which they anoint a shield of leather, making it supple so as to smooth over it the strikes of arrow or spear so that they do not pierce the leather. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 26:11). And I do not know the reason in this, that the Holy One, blessed be He, should say that when we keep all the commandments and do His will, He would not reject us with His soul being repulsed — and likewise when we transgress His covenant and commit great acts of treason, He said, \"I have not rejected them, neither have I repulsed them\" (Leviticus 26:44), and the prophet said at the time of the curse, \"Have You utterly rejected Judah? Has Your soul loathed Zion?\" (Jeremiah 14:19). But the matter is a secret of the hidden things of the Torah. He said that He will set His dwelling among us, and the soul from which the dwelling comes will not repulse us — like a vessel that is scalded with boiling water to expel what is absorbed in it; rather, at all times our garments will be white and new. For <i>ge'ilah</i> — expulsion, in the words of Rashi — is from the language of \"his bull passed and will not expel, his cow will not miscarry\" (Job 21:10). And He said \"My soul\" in the manner of \"Adonai YHWH has sworn by His soul\" (Amos 6:8). And the prophet said in bewilderment: if in Zion itself Your soul has loathed — she who is a city and mother in Israel — and You have cast her from before You so that all her children are clothed in filthy, repulsed garments.\n\nNow these blessings, according to their plain sense — while being comprehensive generalities concerning abundant rain, peace, and fruitfulness — are not like the blessings He already pronounced briefly: \"And I will bless your bread and your waters, and I will remove sickness from your midst\" (Exodus 23:25). For there He promises regarding food and drink and that they will be a source of blessing, so that no illness will befall our bodies, and therefore the organs of generation will be whole and healthy, and we will give birth as is fitting and live full days, as He said: \"None shall be barren or miscarrying in your land; the number of your days I will fulfill\" (Exodus 23:26), and likewise He said beforehand, \"For I am YHWH your healer\" (Exodus 15:26). And the reason for this is that those blessings, although they are miracles, are among the hidden miracles of which the entire Torah is full, as I have explained (Genesis 17:1), and they apply even to an individual who serves Him. For when a pious man keeps all the commandments of YHWH his Elohim, El will guard him from illness, barrenness, and bereavement, and will fill his days with good. But these blessings in this pericah (section) are general to the nation, and they apply when all our people, all of them, are righteous. Therefore He constantly mentions here \"the land\": \"and the land shall yield its fruit,\" \"and you shall dwell securely in your land,\" \"and I will give peace in the land,\" \"from the land,\" \"and no sword shall pass through your land.\" And we have already explained that all these blessings are all miracles — it is not in nature that the rains should come and there should be peace from our enemies and fear fall in their hearts so that they flee a hundred from before five, when we do the statutes and commandments — nor that everything should be the opposite because of our seed in the seventh year. And although they are hidden miracles, with the world proceeding according to its custom, they become publicized from the aspect of their being perpetual, always, throughout the land. For if a single righteous man lives and YHWH removes illness from his midst and fills his days, this too may occur to a few wicked men. But that one entire land and one people should always have rain in its season, and satiety, and tranquility, and peace, and health, and might, and the breaking of enemies — in a manner unlike anything in the entire world — all will know that this was from YHWH. Therefore He said: \"And all the peoples of the earth shall see that the name of YHWH is called upon you, and they shall fear you\" (Deuteronomy 28:10). And the opposite of this will occur in the curses with the punishments of the land, as He said: \"and I will make your sky like iron\" (Leviticus 26:19), and the punishments of illness, as He said: \"and severe and faithful diseases\" (Deuteronomy 28:59) — that He will curse the food and make it disease-causing, and the miracle will become publicized by its being constantly fulfilled among all of them. Therefore it is written: \"And the later generation, your children who will arise after you, and the foreigner who will come from a distant land, and they will see the plagues of that land and its diseases\" (Deuteronomy 29:21) — that man upon whom the entire curse has lain (Deuteronomy 29:19) will not be astonished, for so will it happen many times, according to the way of the world, among all the nations, that evil occurrences befall a single individual. But in that land all the nations will marvel and ask: \"Why has YHWH done thus to this land?\" (Deuteronomy 29:23), for all will see and know that the hand of YHWH has done this, and they will say: \"Because they forsook the covenant of YHWH, the Elohim of their fathers\" (Deuteronomy 29:24).\n\nAnd the general principle is: when Israel is whole and they are many, their affairs are not conducted according to nature at all — neither in their bodies nor in their land, neither in their collective nor in any individual among them. For HaShem will bless their bread and their waters and remove illness from their midst, so that they will not need a physician nor need to guard themselves by any of the ways of medicine whatsoever, as He said: \"For I am YHWH your healer\" (Exodus 15:26). And so the righteous acted in the time of prophecy: even when sin overtook them and they fell ill, they did not seek out physicians but only prophets, as in the case of Hezekiah in his illness (II Kings 20:2–3). And Scripture says: \"Even in his illness he did not seek YHWH but among physicians\" (II Chronicles 16:12). And if the practice of physicians had been customary among them, why would Scripture mention physicians? The guilt would be only for not seeking HaShem. But it is as if one were to say: so-and-so did not eat matzah on the Festival of Unleavened Bread but only leaven. Rather, one who seeks HaShem through the prophet does not seek physicians — and what portion do physicians have in the house of those who do the will of HaShem, after He promised: \"And I will bless your bread and your waters, and I will remove illness from your midst\" (Exodus 23:25)? And the physicians, their practice is only regarding food and drink — to caution against them and to give instructions concerning them. And so they said (Berakhot 60b–64a): for all twenty-two years that Rabbah served as head [of the academy], Rav Yosef did not even call a craftsman to his house. And the parable for them (Numbers Rabbah 9:13): a door that is not open for the commandment is open for the physician. And it is their saying (Berakhot 60a) that the way of human beings is not with medicines, but they became accustomed; for were it not that their way was with medicines, a person would fall ill only to the extent that the punishment for his sin rests upon him, and would be healed by the will of HaShem. But they became accustomed to medicines, and HaShem left them to natural occurrences. And this is their intent in saying (Berakhot 60a): \"and he shall surely heal\" — from here permission was given to the physician to heal. They did not say that permission was given to the sick person to be healed. Rather, since the sick person fell ill and came to be healed — for he had become accustomed to medicines, and he was not of the assembly of HaShem whose portion is in life — the physician is not permitted to refrain from his healing, neither out of concern lest he die by his hand since he is skilled in that craft, nor because he says that HaShem alone is the healer of all flesh, for this has already become customary. Therefore, regarding the men who strove and struck one another with a stone or a fist (Exodus 21:18), there is upon the striker the payment of healing, for the Torah does not base its laws on miracles, as He said: \"For the poor shall not cease from the midst of the land\" (Deuteronomy 15:11) — according to His knowledge that so it will be. But for one whose ways please HaShem, he has no dealings with physicians.\n\nn: The Aramaic parable from Numbers Rabbah is rendered literally; its cryptic force is preserved without expansion. Ramban's citation \"(ברכות ס)\" for Berakhot 60a and \"(ברכות סד)\" for Berakhot 64a follow the page-number convention of the Vilna Talmud."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:32.125930243Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}